  "track": "track-09",
  "title": "cloud computing foundations and architecture",
  "status": "source-backed-scope-complete",
  "concepts": [
    "A cloud account is an administrative boundary for identity, policy, billing, and resource organization; it is distinct from a workload resource group or network.",
    "Cloud providers operate physical facilities and managed control planes, while customers configure workload identity, data, network exposure, application behavior, and the parts of the stack assigned to them by the service model.",
    "Regions and availability zones are geographic and failure-domain concepts, but their exact meanings and service support vary by provider and service. A regional label alone does not guarantee zone redundancy.",
    "IaaS, PaaS, SaaS, and serverless describe responsibility and abstraction boundaries, not a ranking. Managed services transfer some operations while leaving configuration, data, identity, and application responsibilities with the customer.",
    "Cloud architecture composes identity, network, compute, storage, data, messaging, and observability. Managed building blocks reduce some operational work but create provider-specific failure, portability, and cost tradeoffs.",
    "Consumption pricing makes architecture choices visible in recurring cost: capacity, storage tier and operations, requests, data transfer, redundancy, idle resources, and support plans all affect total cost."
  ],
